Counting the number of cycles of a certain length can be achieved using random walks as follows (see Lemma 2.5).,
"Given a d-regular graph, note that the number of walks of length ℓstarting at u is precisely dℓ, and so the probability that a simple random walk that starts in u is in v after ℓsteps is equal to the number of walks from u to v of length ℓdivided by dℓ.",
"Since simple random walks are RANDOM PERFECT MATCHINGS IN REGULAR GRAPHS 3 rapidly mixing in robust expanders, one can precisely estimate such probabilities, and therefore the number of such walks.",
"A simple counting argument can eliminate those walks which are not paths, and so we can accurately count the number of cycles of ﬁxed length in a regular robust expander.",
"In practice, we have to consider simple random walks that use in every second step an edge from a ﬁxed perfect matching M. However, this additional technicality does not aﬀect the mixing properties of such walks and so we can still precisely count them.",
"We remark that Spiro and Surya [10] also used the switching method, which is common for this type of problems.",
"Our contribution is to use longer cycles and perform the analysis with Markov chains; although the intuition is that the estimations become less precise with larger cycles, we employ key properties of Markov chains to show that in fact the opposite is true.",
"Besides the fact that our results are substantially more general, the analysis also becomes signiﬁcantly shorter and cleaner.",
