Lower Bounds on Hitting and Cover times.,
"The lower bounds in the classical version of Matthew’s bound [56, Section 11.2] do not directly (or at least not obviously) imply a lower bound for the cover time of Cα,ν(n) matching the upper bound obtained in Proposition 48.",
"Fortunately, several extensions of Matthew’s bound have been established.",
"Among these, we will take advantage of the following result by Kahn, Kim, Lov´asz and Vu.",
Theorem 49.,
"([42, Theorem 1.3]) If G = (V, E) is a connected graph, then tcov(G) ⩾1 2 · max U⊆V ␀κU · ln |U|  where κU := min u,v∈U[Eu( τv ) + Ev( τu )].",
"The main diﬃculty in applying the preceding result, in order to lower bound the cover time of the center component of the HRG, is ﬁnding an adequate set U of vertices of the center component of Gα,ν(n) which is both suﬃciently large and such that for every distinct pair of elements u, v ∈U the commute time between u and v is large enough, say |U| = nΩ(1) and κU = Ω(ln n).",
"In order to obtain the desired lower bound, we show that such a set of vertices of Cα,ν(n) is likely to exist.",
The set’s existence and the fact that it satisﬁes the necessary conditions relies on a structural result about induced paths of length Θ(log n) in the HRG.,
This result implies that a.a.s.,
"there are many (speciﬁcally, nΩ(1)) vertices of the center component of Gα,ν(n) whose removal gives rise to a path component of order Ω(log n).",
"The result is implicit in [47, Theorem 4.1] and explicitly known (in an equivalent form) to the ﬁrst author and Mitsche since their characterization of the size of the second-largest component of the HRG [49, Theorem 1.1].",
"We state and prove it below, since it is essential for our study of the commute time of the giant component, might also be useful in other settings, and now seems of interest in its own right.",
"The proof argument is somewhat diﬀerent (we believe also simpler) from the one implicit in [47, Theorem 4.1] and relies on the tiling structure constructed in the previous section.",
