"AI prediction leads people to forgo guaranteed rewards Aoi Naito1,2, Hirokazu Shirado1∗ 1Human-Computer Interaction Institute, Carnegie Mellon University, Pittsburgh & 15213, USA.",
"2School of Environment and Society, Institute of Science Tokyo, Tokyo & 108-0023, Japan.",
∗Corresponding author.,
Email: shirado@cmu.edu Artificial intelligence (AI) is understood to affect the content of people’s deci- sions.,
"Here, using a behavioral implementation of the classic Newcomb’s paradox in 1,305 participants, we show that AI can also change how people decide.",
"In this paradigm, belief in predictive authority can lead individuals to constrain decision- making, forgoing a guaranteed reward.",
Over 40% of participants treated AI as such a predictive authority.,
"This significantly increased the odds of forgoing the guaranteed reward by a factor of 3.39 (95% CI: 2.45–4.70) compared with ran- dom framing, and reduced earnings by 10.7–42.9%.",
The effect appeared across AI presentations and decision contexts and persisted even when predictions failed.,
"When people believe AI can predict their behavior, they may self-constrain it in anticipation of that prediction.",
Artificial intelligence (AI) is described as augmenting human cognition (1).,
"AI tools have been shown to increase individual and group productivity (2–4), but also introduce risks such as reduced autonomy (5), increased dishonesty (6), and strategic manipulation (7, 8).",
"Across both positive and negative outcomes, these effects are typically interpreted within a classical Homo economicus framework, in which individuals use AI outputs to choose options that maximize benefits and minimize cost (9, 10).",
"From this perspective, AI is understood to help individuals decide what to 1 arXiv:2603.28944v1 [cs.HC] 30 Mar 2026 do within a given choice structure (11).",
"However, AI may also shape how people decide when it is perceived as predictive.",
This possibility remains poorly understood and challenges standard assumptions that AI informs human decision-making while leaving its underlying decision schema unchanged.,
"This can occur when individuals perceive predictive systems as accurate and overgeneralize this capability, treating them as revealing what they will do (12,13).",
"Decision-making can then shift toward forming intentions consistent with anticipated predictions, which are then enacted (14–17).",
"AI may activate—and amplify—this phenomenon, which we term predictive binding, at times leading individuals to forgo guaranteed rewards.",
"To test this, we conduct behavioral experiments using a decision paradigm based on Newcomb’s paradox (13,18,19), a two-choice task in which different reasoning approaches can lead to different choices (see Supplementary Text).",
Participants face two boxes (Fig.,
"1A), where Box A always contains a guaranteed US$1 and Box B contains either US$0 or US$3.",
"They choose either to take both boxes (two-boxing) or only Box B (one-boxing), and are paid accordingly.",
"Before making their choice, they are told that an AI system predicts which option they will choose.",
"If the AI predicts one-boxing, Box B contains US$3; if it predicts two-boxing, Box B contains US$0.",
"At the time of choice, participants are not told the prediction or the resulting content of Box B.",
"Instead, they are informed that Box B’s content has already been determined by the AI’s prediction and cannot be changed.",
"In our implementation, we do not provide information about the AI’s predictive reliability to examine how participants’ own interpretations of its predictive capability guide their choices.",
"From a strategic dominance perspective, two-boxing yields a higher payoff regardless of predic- tion (1 + 𝑋> 𝑋, where 𝑋∈{0, 3}).",
"However, if individuals treat the AI’s prediction as reflecting their future action, they may instead treat the value of 𝑋as determined by their anticipated choice.",
"Under this reasoning, one-boxing can be preferred, even at the cost of forgoing the guaranteed reward (1 + 0 < 3).",
"Unlike cooperation or social dilemma games (20–22), this paradigm contains no strategic or social incentives that would otherwise justify choosing a lower-payoff option.",
"Thus, observing one-boxing indicates that AI prediction can itself alter how individuals evaluate and select among available actions, even when it does not affect objective payoffs.",
"Using this design, we conducted four preregistered online studies with 1,305 unique participants (see Methods).",
Study 1 (𝑁= 200) tested whether AI prediction increases one-boxing relative to 2 a random control.,
Study 2 (𝑁= 601) examined the robustness of this effect and its underlying mechanism across different interaction contexts and interfaces.,
Study 3 (𝑁= 303) tested whether the effect generalizes beyond the economic task using vignette-based scenarios.,
Study 4 (𝑁= 201) investigated how repeated interaction with AI prediction alters behavior observed in the one-shot task.,
"Results AI prediction increases forgoing guaranteed rewards Across Studies 1 and 2, participants frequently forwent the guaranteed US$1 by choosing one- boxing when the decision was framed as being predicted by an AI system.",
Study 1 tested this effect in a two-condition online experiment (𝑁= 200).,
"Participants were told that the content of Box B would be determined either by an “AI system” predicting their choice or by a “random picker wheel” with the same payoff structure, but without using any prediction of their choice.",
"In the AI condition, 41 of 100 participants (41.0%) chose one-boxing, compared with 26 of 100 (26.0%) in the random condition (𝑝= 0.025; Fig.",
Study 2 (𝑁= 601) confirmed the robustness of this effect across different interaction contexts and system interfaces.,
"In addition to the identity of the predictor (AI versus random mechanism), we manipulated whether participants interacted with the system before making their decision.",
"In the interactive AI condition, participants exchanged brief messages with an AI system powered by OpenAI’s GPT-4.1, whereas the interactive random condition presented a random drawing process of equivalent duration.",
"Non-interactive conditions removed all system-specific interaction and described the outcome as determined by either an “AI system” or a “random generator.” Under random framing, one-boxing remained rare (15.3% in both interactive and non-interactive conditions; Fig.",
"In contrast, one-boxing was substantially more frequent when decisions were framed as predicted by AI (45.0% in the non-interactive AI condition and 42.0% in the interactive AI condition).",
"The overall increase in one-boxing under AI prediction was statistically significant (𝑝< 0.001), whereas interaction with the system did not significantly moderate the effect (𝑝= 1.0 for both random and AI conditions; Table S1).",
"3 A fixed-effect meta-analysis across Studies 1 and 2 confirmed this effect: AI prediction increased the odds of forgoing the guaranteed reward by a factor of 3.39 (odds ratio = 3.39, 95% CI [2.45, 4.70], 𝑝< 0.001; see Supplementary Text).",
This shift had measurable economic consequences.,
"The observed shift toward one-boxing reduced realized earnings by 10.7–42.9% relative to the two-boxing baseline, depending on the AI prediction regime.",
"To examine whether this effect generalizes beyond the stylized economic task, Study 3 presented participants (𝑁= 303) with three vignette scenarios adapted from Newcomb’s paradox: a job interview decision, a mobile data coupon choice, and a task application on a freelancing platform.",
"Each scenario was presented under three conditions: no prediction, human-expert prediction, and AI prediction.",
"Across scenarios, participants chose the one-box option in 26.7% of the cases under AI prediction and 36.6% under human-expert prediction, compared to 10.6% without prediction (Fig.",
"Pairwise contrasts confirmed that both AI and human-expert predictions significantly increased one-box-type choices relative to control (𝑝< 0.001 for both conditions), with a modestly larger effect for human-expert than AI prediction (odds ratio = 1.67, 𝑝= 0.032).",
"While the direction of the effect was consistent across scenarios, its magnitude varied (Fig.",
Mechanism: perceived predictiveness and internal coherence Why did AI prediction make participants forgo guaranteed rewards?,
"The system neither revealed its prediction nor provided any recommendation at the time of choice, ruling out explicit AI persuasion (7, 8).",
"Moreover, identical payoff structures framed around random processes did not produce comparable behavior, indicating that the effect is not driven by payoff structure alone.",
"Building on the causal interpretation of Newcomb’s paradox (19), one-boxing can emerge when participants treat the AI’s prediction and their own action as linked through a representation of “what I will do” (Fig.",
Participants may assume that the system predicts their anticipated action (perceived predictiveness) and treat their eventual action as the realization of that anticipation (internal coherence).,
"When both are strong, prediction and action become psychologically coupled.",
This predictive binding yields outcome bundles—one-boxing with a full Box B (payoff of US$3) or two-boxing with an empty Box B (payoff of US$1)—making one-boxing appear reasonable.,
"Consistent with this account, post-decision evaluations in Study 2 show that participants in the 4 AI conditions perceived the system’s predictions to be significantly more accurate than chance (non- interactive AI: 62.1%, 𝑝< 0.001; interactive AI: 62.9%, 𝑝< 0.001), whereas participants in the random conditions perceived chance-level accuracy (non-interactive random: 50.4%, 𝑝= 0.322; interactive random: 49.7%, 𝑝= 0.639; Fig.",
"These evaluations were collected after choice but before outcome disclosure, making post-hoc justification unlikely.",
"Perceived predictiveness alone, however, did not account for the observed forgoing of the guaranteed reward.",
Estimated predictive accuracy did not differ between participants who chose one-boxing and those who chose two-boxing in the AI conditions (non-interactive AI: 𝑝= 0.080; interactive AI: 𝑝= 0.634).,
This pattern points to the role of internal coherence in driving one- boxing.,
"A computational model based on subjective expected utility theory (23) further supports this interpretation, showing that models incorporating both perceived predictiveness and internal coherence best explain the observed behavior (see Supplementary Text).",
Exploratory analyses in Studies 1 and 2 examined individual characteristics associated with one- boxing (Fig.,
"Sociodemographic variables and beliefs about free will or determinism, as well as affective attitudes toward AI, showed no association with choice in the task.",
"The only individual characteristics associated with one-boxing were higher AI literacy (𝑝= 0.011)—encompassing awareness, usage, and evaluation of AI systems—and greater risk-taking preference (𝑝= 0.003).",
This pattern is inconsistent with explanations attributing the effect to limited understanding of AI.,
Robustness under repeated interaction A defining feature of Newcomb’s paradox is that the decision is made before the prediction is revealed.,
"In real-world settings, however, people often encounter predictive systems repeatedly and receive feedback about prior outcomes.",
We therefore conducted Study 4 (𝑁= 201) to examine how repeated interaction with AI prediction influences the behavioral effect observed in the one-shot task.,
"Participants completed the same choice task over five consecutive rounds, receiving feedback after each round about the AI’s prediction, their own choice, and the resulting outcome.",
"Participants were randomly assigned to one of two conditions: in one condition the AI consistently predicted one- boxing, whereas in the other it consistently predicted two-boxing.",
"Participants were not informed 5 of the prediction policy, and the system did not update its predictions during the task.",
Behavior diverged depending on the AI’s prediction policy (interaction 𝑝= 0.002; Table S2; Fig.,
"When the AI consistently predicted one-boxing, the proportion of one-boxing remained stable across rounds (slope 𝑝= 0.763).",
"In contrast, when the AI consistently predicted two-boxing, the proportion of one-boxing declined significantly over time (slope 𝑝< 0.001).",
"As a result, the AI’s apparent accuracy significantly increased in the two-boxing prediction condition—from 48.5% to 69.3% (𝑝= 0.006)—even though the prediction rule remained fixed and the system did not learn.",
"The increase in accuracy, therefore, arose from behavioral adaptation by participants.",
"This pattern reveals a self-fulfilling dynamic consistent with predictive binding, in which perceived predictiveness reshapes behavior, making predictions appear increasingly accurate (12).",
"Notably, even after five consecutive predictive failures in participants’ choices—when the AI repeatedly predicted two-boxing—the proportion of one-boxing in the final round (30.6%) remained significantly higher than in the random condition of Study 2 (15.3%; 𝑝= 0.003).",
Participants who continued to choose one-boxing under this condition also reported perceived predictive accuracy above chance (Fig.,
"Together, these results indicate that perceived predictiveness can continue to shape decisions even in the face of explicit and repeated disconfirmation.",
Discussion AI prediction might not merely forecast human behavior—it can reshape how people decide.,
"Across four studies, the mere presence of AI prediction led participants to forgo a guaranteed reward, even in the absence of strategic incentives or social pressure.",
This pattern generalized across different AI presentations and across both economically incentivized and vignette-based decision contexts.,
"The effect also remained detectable under repeated interaction, even when the system repeatedly failed to predict some participants’ behavior.",
"Together, these results suggest that, under certain conditions, AI prediction can change how people decide, not just the outcomes they pursue.",
We do not interpret this pattern as irrationality.,
"Forgoing guaranteed rewards can be rational even from an expected utility perspective when prediction and action are perceived as coupled (18,19,24).",
"One interpretation is that predictive authority changes decision-making by making future behavior 6 feel already partially settled, so that it functions like intention and guides action.",
"People may therefore align what they believe they “will” do with what they believe the system predicts (15), even when this conflicts with immediate economic incentives.",
The dual meaning of “will” as both future realization and volitional commitment may reflect how people naturally link anticipation and intention (25).,
"This reasoning produces an apparent reversal of causality in which ex-post action follows ex-ante prediction (12,26).",
This mechanism of predictive binding has important implications for human agency (27).,
"In our experiments, once the prediction was generated, participants remained objectively free to choose the higher-payoff option.",
"Nevertheless, the prior prediction may already have shaped how they decided, even without conscious awareness (5).",
"Responsibility may then become psychologically entangled with the predictive system, rather than experienced as fully self-generated (28,29).",
Predictive binding is not unique to AI.,
"Human behavior is often organized around anticipated expectations—of institutions, communities, audiences, or imagined observers (14, 17, 30, 31).",
"In Study 3, human-expert prediction showed even stronger effects than AI, suggesting that predictive binding may extend beyond machine systems and interact with existing forms of social authority.",
"AI is nevertheless distinctive in how it makes such expectations newly empirical, legible, and scalable (32).",
"Unlike purely symbolic predictors, AI systems are widely understood as statistical tools that can infer future behavior from past data across many domains (33–35).",
Prior work also shows that people may adjust their behavior to shape AI through training (26).,
Our findings suggest a complementary dynamic around AI prediction.,
"In our experiments, participants were not given information about the predictive capability of the AI system.",
"Despite that, they inferred its predictive authority on their own and adjusted their decisions in anticipation of it.",
This gives AI prediction a distinctive plausibility.,
This possibility bears directly on collective action (36).,
"Social dilemmas require individuals to forgo immediate gains in anticipation of what others—or a larger system—will do (20,37,38).",
"Predictive systems may therefore influence collective outcomes not only by informing strategic decisions, but by shaping whether people experience restraint or cooperation as what one is supposed to do (30,39,40).",
"In this sense, predictive binding could either support or undermine cooperation, depending on which behaviors are expected, legitimized, or stabilized in a given environment (41, 42).",
This suggests a broader research agenda for hybrid human–machine societies (43–45) 7 because AI reasoning may act as a social force that organizes behavior around what is expected (46).,
"Our study intentionally isolates a minimal decision environment, allowing us to identify a basic behavioral mechanism under tightly controlled conditions.",
"This design necessarily omits many features of real-world choice, including strategic interaction, social norms, and cultural and historical context (17,20,40).",
We therefore do not claim that the effect observed here directly generalizes to open social environments where these forces interact.,
"At the same time, this simplicity is a strength: it shows that predictive binding can emerge even in the absence of social enforcement, institutional rules, or actual predictive capabilities.",
"In our experiments, the AI systems did not perform any algorithmic prediction; the observed effects therefore reflect human psychology alone.",
"Comparable effects across multiple AI pre- sentations—a virtual agent, an LLM-based assistant, and the label “AI” alone—suggest that the phenomenon depends less on technical sophistication than on perceived predictive authority.",
Fu- ture work should examine how these psychological effects interact with predictive systems that demonstrably anticipate behavior.,
"Real-world AI systems with verifiable predictive accuracy may elicit stronger or more persistent beliefs, potentially amplifying the effects observed here.",
"As AI becomes commonplace, its influence may lie not only in what it predicts, but in how prediction reshapes decision-making itself.",
A culture with machines may affect the culture of humans.,
"Our results highlight prediction as a source of behavioral influence that can operate without coercion, recommendation, or incentive change.",
"Addressing the societal impact of AI will therefore require attention not only to what AI predicts or does, but also to how perceived predictive capabilities reshape the decisions people experience as their own.",
