S35 Statistical analyses A fixed-effect meta-analysis across Studies 1 and 2.,
"To evaluate the overall effect of the AI condition on participants’ decisions across Studies 1 and 2, we conducted a fixed-effect meta- analysis.",
"First, we independently fitted logistic regression models for both studies.",
"The dependent variable was the binary choice in the task (1 = one-boxing, 0 = two-boxing), and the independent variable was the identity of the predictor (1 = AI, 0 = random).",
"Next, we pooled the extracted log-odds coefficients (𝛽) and their standard errors (𝑆𝐸) from both models using a fixed-effects meta-analysis with inverse-variance weighting.",
"The pooled standard error (𝑆𝐸𝑚𝑒𝑡𝑎), 𝑍-score, and the corresponding 𝑝-value were calculated to assess the overall statis- tical significance.",
The pooled effect size was then converted into an odds ratio (OR) with a 95% confidence interval (CI).,
"Finally, to examine the consistency of the AI effect across the two studies, we assessed between- study heterogeneity using Cochran’s 𝑄statistic and the 𝐼2 index.",
Competitive ratio of realized earnings to theory boundaries under AI regimes.,
"To evaluate the observed shift toward one-boxing reduced realized earnings in Studies 1 and 2, we calculated competitive ratio of realized to theoretical earnings under AI regimes.",
"The lower bound corresponds to a prediction regime in which the AI always predicts one-boxing (yielding payoffs of 3 or 4), whereas the upper bound corresponds to a regime in which the AI always predicts two-boxing (yielding payoffs of 0 or 1).",
Computational modeling of decision-making Model definition.,
"To examine how perceived predictiveness and internal coherence contribute to participants’ actions in Study 2, we introduced a computational model of the decision-making process grounded in subjective expected utility theory (23).",
"Let 𝐴denote the participant’s choice (one-boxing or two-boxing), 𝑃denote the system’s pre- diction of 𝐴, and 𝐴𝐴denote the participant’s anticipated action.",
Perceived predictiveness is defined as 𝑝= Pr(𝑃= 𝐴𝐴) and internal coherence as 𝑞= Pr(𝐴= 𝐴𝐴).,
"In this formulation, 𝑝represents the perceived probability that the system’s prediction matches the participant’s anticipated action, S36 and 𝑞represents the extent to which participants treat their eventual action as coupled to their anticipated action (Fig.",
"Box A (transparent) always yields payoff 𝑆, whereas Box B (opaque) yields either 0 or 𝐿 depending on the system’s prediction 𝑃, with 0 < 𝑆< 𝐿(Fig.",
Participants are assumed to evaluate actions using subjective expected utility.,
"The expected utilities of one-boxing and two-boxing, denoted by 𝐸𝑈[1box] and 𝐸𝑈[2box], depend on participants’ internal coherence Pr(𝐴= 𝐴𝐴).",
"If participant 𝑖assumes no anticipation–action coupling (i.e., 𝐴≠𝐴𝐴), then the chosen action provides no information about the participant’s anticipated action.",
"Under this assumption, the expected payoff from Box B is the same for one-boxing and two-boxing and depends only on the perceived predictiveness parameter 𝑝𝑖.",
The expected utilities become 𝐸𝑈[1box | 𝐴≠𝐴𝐴] = 𝑝𝑖𝐿 (S1) 𝐸𝑈[2box | 𝐴≠𝐴𝐴] = 𝑆+ 𝑝𝑖𝐿 (S2) Δ𝐸𝑈independent = 𝐸𝑈[1box] −𝐸𝑈[2box] = −𝑆.,
"(S3) Under this model, one-boxing is never preferred.",
"If participant 𝑖assumes that anticipated action and realized action are perfectly coherent (i.e., 𝐴= 𝐴𝐴), then the chosen action fully reveals the participant’s anticipated action.",
"Under this assumption, the expected content of Box B depends on both perceived predictiveness 𝑝𝑖and the participant’s choice.",
The expected utilities become 𝐸𝑈[1box | 𝐴= 𝐴𝐴] = 𝑝𝑖𝐿 (S4) 𝐸𝑈[2box | 𝐴= 𝐴𝐴] = 𝑆+ (1 −𝑝𝑖)𝐿 (S5) Δ𝐸𝑈coherent = 2𝑝𝑖𝐿−𝐿−𝑆.,
"(S6) S37 Under this model, one-boxing is preferred whenever 𝑝𝑖> (𝐿+ 𝑆)/(2𝐿).",
"Under these assumptions, we evaluated four candidate models of decision-making: (i) a ran- dom baseline model, (ii) an independent-action model, (iii) an anticipated–action coherent model, and (iv) a system-modulated anticipated–action coherent model.",
"Each model specifies different assumptions about perceived predictiveness and internal coherence, as described below.",
Random action model.,
"As a baseline, we assume that participants choose randomly between the two options with equal probability, independent of prediction or anticipation: Pr(1box) = 0.5.",
(S7) Independent action model.,
"In the independent action model, we assume that participants ignore any coherence between anticipated action and realized action (i.e., 𝑞= 0).",
Decisions are therefore based only on the expected utility difference in Eq.,
The probability of choosing one-boxing is Pr(1box) = 𝜎(𝜏· Δ𝐸𝑈independent) (S8) where 𝜎(𝑥) = 1 1+𝑒−𝑥is a logistic function and 𝜏> 0 is an inverse temperature parameter capturing sensitivity to the expected utility difference.,
Internal coherence model.,
"In the internal coherence model, we assume that participants perceive perfect coherence between their anticipated and realized actions (i.e., 𝑞= 1).",
Decisions are therefore based on the perceived predictiveness 𝑝𝑖and the expected utility difference in Eq.,
The probability of choosing one-boxing is Pr(1box) = 𝜎(𝜏· Δ𝐸𝑈coherent).,
(S9) System-mediated internal coherence model.,
"In the system-mediated internal coherence model, we extend the internal coherence model by allowing the strength of internal coherence to vary depending on the presence of the system’s prediction.",
"We specify the internal coherence 𝑞as a condition-specific parameter 𝑞𝑐, where 𝑐∈{AI, random} denotes the experimental condition.",
"S38 The parameter 𝑞𝑐∈[0, 1] represents the probability that a participant evaluates the decision using the coherent model rather than the independent model.",
The resulting choice probability is given by Pr(1box) = 𝑞𝑐𝜎(𝜏· Δ𝐸𝑈coherent) + (1 −𝑞𝑐) 𝜎(𝜏· Δ𝐸𝑈independent).,
(S10) Parameter fitting.,
"The observed choice 𝑦𝑖∈{0, 1} (where 1 denotes the one-boxing choice) for participant 𝑖is modeled as a Bernoulli trial drawn from the predicted probability: 𝑦𝑖∼Bernoulli(Pr(1box)).",
(S11) Parameter estimation.,
"Given the between-subject design, in which each participant made a one-shot binary choice, we adopted a Bayesian approach to stabilize parameter estimation.",
"Prior to estimation, perceived predictiveness, originally measured on a scale from 0 to 100, was rescaled to the unit interval [0, 1].",
"To regularize estimation while remaining weakly informative, we assigned weakly informative priors to all free parameters.",
"The priors were specified as follows: 𝜏∼HalfNormal(2) (S12) 𝑞𝑐∼Beta(2, 2), 𝑐∈{AI, random}.",
"(S13) Posterior distributions were estimated using the No-U-Turn Sampler (NUTS), a variant of Hamiltonian Monte Carlo, implemented in PyMC (v5.27.0).",
"We ran four parallel chains with 2,000 iterations each, discarding the first 1,000 iterations of each chain as warmup, yielding 4,000 posterior samples.",
"Convergence was assessed using the Gelman–Rubin statistic ( ˆ𝑅), and all parameters satisfied ˆ𝑅< 1.01.",
Modeling results.,
"First, we compared the goodness-of-fit of the four candidate models using LOO, WAIC, and WBIC (Table S3).",
The system-mediated internal coherence model provided the best fit across all metrics.,
"This result suggests that variation in perceived predictiveness alone S39 cannot fully account for the observed choice patterns, and that incorporating an internal coherence component improves the model’s explanatory power.",
"To further examine how the presence of system’s prediction influenced participants’ behavior, we inspected the posterior distributions of the parameters in the winning model.",
"The inverse temperature parameter (𝜏) was estimated at 0.263 (95% HDI: [0.216, 0.315]), indicating that participants’ choices were systematically related to the expected utility differences implied by the model.",
We then compared the estimated internal-coherence parameters for the AI condition (𝑞𝐴𝐼) and the random control condition (𝑞𝑟𝑎𝑛𝑑𝑜𝑚).,
"The posterior mean of 𝑞𝐴𝐼was 0.682 (95% HDI: [0.425, 0.927]), whereas the posterior mean of 𝑞𝑟𝑎𝑛𝑑𝑜𝑚was lower at 0.443 (95% HDI: [0.076, 0.836]).",
"However, the posterior probability that 𝑞𝐴𝐼> 𝑞𝑟𝑎𝑛𝑑𝑜𝑚was 82.6%, which does not reach a conventional evidential threshold (e.g., 95%).",
"Together, these results indicate that a model combining perceived predictiveness Pr(𝑃= 𝐴𝐴) with internal coherence Pr(𝐴= 𝐴𝐴) provides the best account of the observed behavior.",
"This pattern is consistent with the proposed predictive-binding mechanism, in which perceived predictiveness interacts with anticipated–action coherence to shape decision evaluation.",
"They further suggest that AI framing may increase the tendency for participants to evaluate decisions through internal coherence, although the present data do not provide decisive statistical evidence for this difference.",
