"MODEL We consider a charging model of the QB, where the signal mode serves as the battery and the pump mode acts as the charger, as illustrated in Fig.",
"Setting ℏ= 1, the total Hamiltonian is written as [48] Htot = Hsys + HB + Hirr, (1) where Hsys = H0 + Hint, (2) H0 = ωsa† sas + ωpa† pap, (3) Hint = iκ 2 a†2 s ap + i√γpa† pFpe−iωpt + h.c., (4) HB = X j=s,p Z ∞ 0 dω ω b† j(ω)bj(ω), (5) Hirr = i√γs a† sBs + i√γp a† pBp + h.c. (6) Here, H0 describes the free Hamiltonian of the DOPO, and a† s (a† p) is the creation operator of the signal (pump) mode with frequency ωs (ωp).",
"The ﬁrst term in Hint describes the nonlinear interaction between the signal and pump modes, where κ denotes the coupling strength responsible for the charging process.",
"The second term represents the coherent driving applied to the pump mode, with Fp being the amplitude of the external classical pump ﬁeld.",
"Since Fp is the amplitude of the injected classical ﬁeld, it satisﬁes Fp ∝ p Pin/(ℏωp) [69], where Pin is the input pump power.",
"Treating the injected ﬁeld as a stable source, we adopt the pump approximation and take Fp to be a constant.",
"In addition, bj(ω) and b† j(ω) are the annihilation and creation operators of the bath mode with frequency ω in channel j ∈{s, p}.",
"The dissipation Hamiltonian Hirr describes the coupling between the system and the environment, where Bj = Z ∞ 0 dω gj(ω)bj(ω), (7) with gj(ω) being the coupling strength between the system mode and the bath mode at frequency ω.",
"The parameters γs and γp characterize the corresponding dissipation rates of the signal and pump modes, respectively.",
"According to Appendix A, the master equation of the system can be written as dρ dt = −i  Heﬀ, ρ  + γs  as ρ a† s −1 2{a† sas, ρ}  + γp  ap ρ a† p −1 2{a† pap, ρ}  , (8) Here, the anti-commutator is deﬁned as {A, B} = AB + BA.",
The ﬁrst term in Eq.,
(8) describes the coherent evolution of the QB governed by the eﬀective Hamiltonian Heﬀ.,
"The second term characterizes the dissipation of the QB induced by the environment with decay rate γs, while the third term accounts for the dissipative loss of the pump mode (charger) with rate γp.",
"The QB is initially prepared in the vacuum state, i.e., |ψs(0)⟩= |0⟩.",
"In the numerical simulations, we adopt the parameters which are fully-consistent with realistic DOPO.",
"In typical experiments [48, 70, 71], γs ≪γp and κ/γs ranges from 0.1 to 1.",
