"In the weak-coupling regime, e.g., g/γs = 0.300, the peak value of κa is relatively low and rises very slowly.",
"This is because the smaller the coupling strength g/γs is, the longer the period of the Rabi oscillations between the QB and the TLS becomes.",
"As a consequent, the time required to complete eﬃcient energy transfer increases accordingly.",
"Meanwhile, the dissipation in the system continuously causes energy loss, leading to a decay over time in the ergotropy stored in the QB.",
"Since the energy transfer takes a certain amount of time to complete, before the TLS absorbs suﬃcient energy from the QB, the ergotropy of the QB has already been signiﬁcantly reduced due to the persistent dissipation, ultimately resulting in a lower peak value of κa and a slow rising process.",
"As g/γs increases, the energy transfer accelerates dramatically, and thus increases the peak value of κa and reduces the time required to reach it markedly.",
"Once g/γs ≳1.00, the maximum κa saturates at approximately 0.43 and stops to grow further with increasing g. This saturation indicates that the transfer eﬃciency is fundamentally limited by the ﬁnite ergotropy Ws(t0) stored in the QB at the instant switching oﬀthe pump, rather than by the coupling strength itself.",
A particularly-important observation is the existence of an optimal discharging time.,
"For a given g, κa(t) exhibits a Rabi-like oscillation.",
"κa(t) reaches maximum at the ﬁrst peak, with later peaks decaying rapidly due to the accumulative dissipation during each energy exchange.",
"Therefore, the highest energy-transfer eﬃciency is achieved by disconnecting the load with the QB precisely at the ﬁrst maximum of κa(t).",
"Combining this insight with the charging stage analysis, particularly Figs.",
"4 and 6, Wc(t) reaches its maximum near γst ≈10 during the charging stage, and this nearly coincides with the maximum of the average charging power.",
"Therefore, γst ≈10 deﬁnes the optimal time to switch oﬀthe pump.",
