"Here, Hsys = H0 + Hint, (A2) where Hsys describes the DOPO system itself, H0 is the free Hamiltonian, and Hint is the interaction Hamiltonian.",
"With H0 = ωsa† sas + ωpa† pap, (A3) where a† s (as) and a† p (ap) are the creation (annihilation) operators of the signal and pump modes with frequencies ωs and ωp, respectively.",
"And Hint = iκ 2 a†2 s ap + i√γp a† pFpe−iωpt + h.c., (A4) where the ﬁrst term describes the nonlinear coupling between the signal and pump modes, with κ being the corresponding coupling strength.",
"And the second term represents the coherent driving on the pump mode, where Fp is the amplitude of the external classical pump ﬁeld and γp is the dissipation rate of the pump mode.",
"The bath Hamiltonian is HB = X j=s,p Z ∞ 0 dω ω b† j(ω)bj(ω).",
"(A5) 9 Here, b† j(ω) (bj(ω)) is the creation (annihilation) operator of the bath mode with frequency ω in channel j ∈{s, p}.",
"The system-bath interaction is Hirr = i√γs a† sBs + i√γp a† pBp + h.c., (A6) where γs and γp characterize the dissipation rates of the signal and pump modes, respectively.",
With Bj = Z ∞ 0 dω gj(ω)bj(ω).,
"(A7) Here, Bj is the collective bath operator for channel j ∈ {s, p}, and gj(ω) denotes the coupling strength between the system mode and the bath mode at frequency ω.",
"Furthermore, we assume the resonance condition 2ωs = ωp.",
"(A8) To derive the reduced dynamics, we move to the interaction picture with respect to the free Hamiltonian H0 + HB.",
"The system operators evolve as aj(t) = aje−iωjt, (A9) a† j(t) = a† jeiωjt, (A10) while the bath operators evolve as bj(ω, t) = bj(ω)e−iωt.",
"(A11) Accordingly, Bj(t) = Z ∞ 0 dω gj(ω)bj(ω)e−iωt.",
"(A12) Under the resonance condition, the interaction Hamiltonian in the interaction picture becomes time independent as Heﬀ= iκ 2 ␀a†2 s ap −a2 sa† p  +i√γp ␀Fpa† p −F ∗ p ap  .",
"(A13) For the sake of simplicity, we assume Fp to be real.",
"The system-bath coupling in the interaction picture is HI irr(t) = i X j=s,p √γja† jeiωjtBj(t) + h.c. (A14) The total density operator in the interaction picture satisﬁes d dtρI tot(t) = −i  Heﬀ+ HI irr(t), ρI tot(t)  .",
"(A15) Under the Born-Markovian approximation, we assume ρI tot(t) ≈ρI S(t) ⊗ρB, (A16) where ρI S(t) is the reduced density operator of the system and ρB is the thermal state of the bath.",
"Tracing out the bath degrees of freedom yields d dtρI(t) = −i [Heﬀ, ρI(t)] − Z ∞ 0 dτ TrB  HI irr(t),  HI irr(t −τ), ρI(t) ⊗ρB  .",
"(A17) For a thermal bath, the correlation functions are ⟨b† j(ω)bk(ω′)⟩= δjkδ(ω −ω′) ¯nj(ω), (A18) and ⟨bj(ω)b† k(ω′)⟩= δjkδ(ω −ω′) [¯nj(ω) + 1] , (A19) where ¯nj(ω) = 1 eβjω −1, (A20) and β = 1/(kBT ), with kB the Boltzmann constant and T the temperature of the thermal bath.",
"Within the Markovian approximation, the dissipative rates are evaluated at the corresponding system frequencies, giving the downhill and uphill rates respectively Γ− j = γj (¯nj + 1) , (A21) Γ+ j = γj¯nj, (A22) with ¯nj = ¯nj(ωj) = 1 eβjωj −1.",
"(A23) Neglecting the Lamb shift, the quantum master equation of the system takes the Lindblad-form as d dtρ = −i [Heﬀ, ρ] + X j=s,p γj (¯nj + 1) D[aj]ρ + X j=s,p γj¯njD[a† j]ρ, (A24) where D[L]ρ = LρL† −1 2L†Lρ −1 2ρL†L.",
"(A25) The terms proportional to ¯nj describe thermal excitation from the environment, while the terms proportional to ¯nj + 1 correspond to decay processes including both spontaneous and stimulated emission.",
"In a typical DOPO, the signal wavelength is λ = 1550 nm, corresponding to an energy Es = hc/λ ≈ 1.28 × 10−19 J.",
"At the room temperature, the thermal energy is kBT ≈4.14 × 10−21 J.",
"Therefore, the thermal occupation number is ¯ns = 1 eEs/kBT −1 ≈3.5 × 10−14 ≪1.",
(A26) A similar estimation holds for the pump mode.,
"Consequently, for typical DOPO parameters at the room temperature, the thermal-excitation terms proportional to ¯ns and ¯np in Eq.",
(A24) are negligibly small and the ﬁnite-temperature master equation eﬀectively reduces to its zero-temperature form.,
"Therefore, under typical DOPO operating conditions, Eq.",
"(A24) is well approximated by the zero-temperature master equation used in the main text as dρ dt = −i  Heﬀ, ρ  + γs  as ρ a† s −1 2{a† sas, ρ}  + γp  ap ρ a† p −1 2{a† pap, ρ}  .",
"(A27) 10 Appendix B: Determination of the Pump Threshold In a CIM, the DOPO has a pump threshold.",
"Below the threshold, the signal ﬁeld mainly exhibits the characteristics of quantum squeezing.",
"Above the threshold, parametric oscillation occurs, and the energy of the optical ﬁeld is further enhanced.",
"For a QB, our goal is to store and extract energy, which requires the DOPO to operate above the pump threshold.",
"Meanwhile, the pump intensity should not be much higher than the pump threshold, as this would damage the optical components.",
"(1), by transforming to the rotating frame with respect to U(t) = exp  iωpt ␀a† pap + 1 2a† sas  , (B1) we obtain the Heisenberg–Langevin equations as [48, 75] d dt˜as = −γs 2 ˜as + κ˜a† s˜ap + √γs ˜Bs, (B2) d dt˜ap = −γp 2 ˜ap −κ 2 ˜a2 s + √γpFp + √γp ˜Bp, (B3) where ˜aj = aj exp(iωjt) and ˜Bj = Bj exp(iωjt) (j = s, p) are the slowly-varying operators.",
"(B2), the ﬁrst term on the right hand side represents the cavity loss, and the second term corresponds to the nonlinear χ(2) process that converts the pump photons into the signal photons, and the third term represents the vacuum ﬂuctuation of the signal ﬁeld.",
"(B3), the ﬁrst term of the right hand side also denotes the cavity loss, and the second term corresponds to the nonlinear conversion of the pump photons into the signal photons, and the third term represents the externally-injected pump drive, and the last term accounts for the vacuum ﬂuctuation of the pump ﬁeld.",
