"Appendix D: Decay of Coherent and Incoherent Ergotropy in a TLS For clarity, in this Appendix we illustrate the deﬁnitions of coherent and incoherent ergotropy by using a TLS as a QB.",
"To keep the notation consistent with the main text, ρTLS(t) denotes the density matrix of the TLS, and ̺TLS(t) is the dephased state obtained from ρTLS(t) by removing all oﬀ-diagonal elements in the energy eigenbasis, and ˜ρTLS(t) is the passive state constructed from ρTLS(t), and ˜̺TLS(t) is the passive state constructed from ̺TLS(t).",
"The incoherent and coherent parts of ergotropy are then deﬁned as W i TLS(t) = Tr[HTLSρTLS(t)]−Tr[HTLS˜̺TLS(t)] , (D1) W c TLS(t) = Tr[HTLS˜̺TLS(t)]−Tr[HTLS˜ρTLS(t)] .",
"(D2) As an illustrative example, we consider a TLS with excited state |e⟩and ground state |g⟩.",
"Its Hamiltonian is HTLS =  0 0 0 ω  , (D3) where ω is the level spacing.",
"The density matrix of the TLS can be written as ρTLS(t) =  1 −p(t) c(t) c∗(t) p(t)  , (D4) where p(t) is the excited-state population and c(t) is the oﬀ-diagonal coherence term.",
"By diagonalizing ρTLS(t), we obtain its eigenvalues λ±(t) = 1 ± q (2p(t) −1)2 + 4|c(t)|2 2 , (D5) and hence the passive state ˜ρTLS(t) =  λ+(t) 0 0 λ−(t)  .",
(D6) The dephased state is ̺TLS(t) =  1 −p(t) 0 0 p(t)  .,
"(D7) The stored energy of the TLS is ETLS(t) = Tr[HTLSρTLS(t)] = ωp(t), (D8) while the passive energy associated with ˜ρTLS(t) is Tr[HTLS˜ρTLS(t)] = ωλ−(t).",
(D9) We now discuss two regimes.,
"When p(t) ≤1/2, the ground-state population is dominant, the dephased state is already passive, i.e., ˜̺TLS(t) =  1 −p(t) 0 0 p(t)  .",
"(D10) Therefore, Tr[HTLS˜̺TLS(t)] = ωp(t), (D11) W i TLS(t) = 0.",
"(D12) In this regime, no incoherent ergotropy can be extracted from the population inversion, and the total ergotropy is entirely given by the coherent part, WTLS(t) = W c TLS(t) = ωp(t) −ωλ−(t).",
"(D13) When p(t) ≥1/2, the populations in the dephased state must be rearranged to form the passive state, i.e., ˜̺TLS(t) =  p(t) 0 0 1 −p(t)  .",
"(D14) In this case, the incoherent part becomes W i TLS(t) = ωp(t) −ω ␀1 −p(t)  = ω ␀2p(t) −1  , (D15) while the coherent part is W c TLS(t) = ω ␀1 −p(t)  −ωλ−(t).",
"(D16) In particular, when the coherence vanishes, i.e., c(t) = 0, one has ρTLS(t) = ̺TLS(t), (D17) ˜ρTLS(t) = ˜̺TLS(t), (D18) and therefore Tr [HTLS˜̺TLS(t)] = Tr [HTLS˜ρTLS(t)] , (D19) W c TLS(t) = 0.",
(D20) This explicitly shows that the coherent part of ergotropy originates from the oﬀ-diagonal coherence term c(t).,
"Next, we examine the eﬀect of dissipation on both coherent and incoherent ergotropy.",
"Under amplitude damping, the Lindblad-form master equation reads [75] dρTLS(t) dt = −i [HTLS, ρTLS(t)] + γs ␀σ−ρTLS(t)σ+ −1 2  σ+σ−, ρTLS(t)  , (D21) where γs is the relaxation rate.",
Substituting Eq.,
"(D4) into the above equation, we obtain ˙p(t) = −γsp(t), (D22) ˙c(t) =  iω −γs 2  c(t).",
"(D23) 12 The corresponding solutions are p(t) = p(0)e−γst, (D24) c(t) = c(0)e(iω−γs 2 )t, (D25) which yields |c(t)|2 = |c(0)|2e−γst.",
"(D26) For p(t) ≥1/2, the incoherent part becomes W i TLS(t) = ω ␀2p(t) −1  = ω ␀2p(0)e−γst −1  .",
"(D27) Meanwhile, the coherent part can be written as W c TLS(t) = ω 2 q (2p(0)e−γst −1)2 + 4|c(0)|2e−γst − 2p(0)e−γst −1  .",
(D28) These expressions provide a simple explanation for the diﬀerent behaviors of the two contributions.,
"Under amplitude damping, the incoherent part W i TLS(t) is fully determined by the population inversion 2p(t) − 1, and therefore decreases rapidly as the excited-state population decays, vanishing once p(t) ≤1/2.",
"By contrast, the coherent part W c TLS(t) remains nonzero as long as the oﬀ-diagonal coherence survives, i.e., c(t) ̸= 0.",
"Therefore, within this amplitude-damping model, the coherent contribution generally exhibits stronger robustness against dissipation than the incoherent one.",
