"The positive implication of the term AC is counterbalanced by the term Potential Distortion, while both being moderated by the term Cost.",
"The term Cost encompasses all costs of the visualization process, including computational costs (e.g., visual mapping and rendering), cognitive costs (e.g., cognitive load), and consequential costs (e.g., impact of errors).",
"As illustrated in Figure 2, increasing AC typically enables the reduction of cost (e.g., in terms of energy, or its approximation such as time or money).",
The term Potential Distortion (PD) measures the informative di- vergence between viewing the data through visualization with in- formation loss and reading the data without any information loss.,
"The latter might be ideal but is usually at an unattainable cost ex- cept for values in a very small data space (i.e., in a small alphabet as discussed in [CG16]).",
"As shown in Figure 2, increasing AC typ- ically causes more PD.",
PD is data-dependent or user-dependent.,
"Given the same data visualization with the same amount of infor- mation loss, one can postulate that a user with more knowledge about the data or visual representation usually suffers less distor- tion.",
This postulation is a focus of this paper.,
