"Let us give some comments on the proof of Theorem 1.3, which will be carried out in Subsection 3.2: Throughout the proof we will make heavy use of Theorem 1.2 above.",
"Proving that (1.5) is a sufficient condition for the embedding W 1,p ω (Ω) ,→W 1,p(Ω) (for any p ∈[1, +∞]) follows immediately from the fact that the weak nonlocal gradient induced by ω of any function in W 1,p(Ω) is ω times the weak nonlocal gradient induced by 1 of the function, see Remark 2.5.",
"Showing that condition (1.6) implies W 1,p(Ω) ,→ W 1,p ω (Ω) is also straightforward and follows from an integral/supremum inequality.",
"Instead, the bulk of the proof of Theorem 1.3 will be dedicated to proving that (1.6) is also a necessary condition for the aforementioned embedding.",
"To do this in the p ∈[1, +∞) case, for a given x0 ∈Ωwe will construct a family of test functions whose nonlocal Sobolev seminorm tends to fp ω(x0), see Lemma 3.7.",
"This will only give us the embeddings “seminorm-wise” (see Proposition 3.8), and additionally we will have to check that the Lp norms of the test functions tend to zero.",
"To prove the p = +∞case, a different argument involving the same test functions will be used.",
"Since this procedure essentially relies on test functions, the argument can be adapted to prove the corresponding part of Theorem 1.1 with p = 1 using the fact that NLTVω coincides with the nonlocal Sobolev seminorm for weakly derivable functions, see Proposition 2.21.",
"On another note, it may come as a surprise that, despite our weight functions being C1, the space of smooth functions with compact support may not be included in the nonlocal Sobolev/BV space for some choices of ω.",
"What is more, certain weight functions induce nonlocal spaces for which the null function is the only C∞ c function contained in them.",
The weight functions which ensure that this is not the case are characterized as follows: Theorem 1.4 (Relation with the space of test functions).,
"Let Ωbe a nonempty open subset of RN, p ∈ [1, +∞) and ω be an admissible weight function in Ω.",
"Then, we have that W 1,p ω (Ω) ∩C∞ c (Ω) ̸= {0} if and only if there exists a compact set K ⊂Ωwith nonempty interior such that Z K Z Ω ω(x, y)pdydx < +∞.",
