"If p = 1, the same statements hold when W 1,1 ω (Ω) is replaced by NLBVω(Ω).",
The proof of Theorem 1.4 will be given in Section 4.,
"The argument is based on the fact that the nonlocal Sobolev seminorm induced by ω of a (locally) weakly derivable function u splits into two integrals (or essential supremums if p = +∞) depending on ∇u and ω; one dealing with the interactions within ess supp(u) × ess supp(u), and the other dealing with the interactions in ess supp(u) × (Ω\ ess supp(u)), see Remark 4.1.",
"We will see that for test functions the first integral/supremum is always finite, and this will imply, after some arguments, that any test function belongs to the nonlocal Sobolev space if and only if the second integral/supremum is finite, see Lemma 4.2.",
"After seeing that this second term is finite if and only if (1.7)/(1.8) holds, the proof will follow.",
"Once the Sobolev case is proven, the NLBVω statements will follow using Proposition 2.21.",
"Notice that, in contrast to Theorems 1.1, 1.2 and 1.3, Theorem 1.4 above does not require that Ωhave finite measure, even for p ̸= +∞.",
It is here that we point out why we ask that the domain (i.e.,
"Ω) be of finite measure instead of being bounded, which is a more standard (yet restrictive) condition.",
"The reason is that, for p ∈[1, +∞), in the aforementioned results we are essentially only working with integrals, and to bound some of these integrals it is sufficient to ask that Ωhas finite measure.",
"This being said, on some results we do ask that Ωbe bounded, and this is due to the fact that their proofs pass through classical Sobolev and BV results which require it.",
"With this in mind, let us investigate the case in which Ωhas infinite measure.",
"It turns out that, under such an assumption, the landscape changes dramatically, and for some choices of weights one is lead to (possibly) trivial spaces.",
"Indeed, as an immediate corollary of Theorem 1.4 we have: Corollary 1.5.",
"Assume that Ωis a nonempty open subset of RN that has infinite measure and let p ∈ [1, +∞) and ω be an admissible weight function in Ω.",
"If there exists a constant c > 0 such that for all (x, y) ∈Ω× Ωone has c ≤ω(x, y), then C∞ c (Ω) ∩W 1,p ω (Ω) = C∞ c (Ω) ∩NLBVω(Ω) = {0}.",
"The proof of the corollary above readily follows from noticing that, if ω is bounded away from zero and Ωis of infinite measure, the quantity (1.7) is infinite regardless of K (assuming it has nonempty interior).",
"It is natural to conjecture that in the case in which W 1,p ω (Ω) ∩C∞ c (Ω) = {0} one actually has W 1,p ω (Ω) = {0}, and analogously for NLBVω(Ω).",
"This would easily follow from Corollary 1.5 combined with density and approximation results for W 1,p ω (Ω) or NLBVω(Ω) with respect to compactly supported smooth functions.",
"We have not been able to prove such results, essentially due to the fact that we do not have an appropriate product rule for the nonlocal operators divω and ∇ω (for more comments on approximation results in our setting, we direct the reader to the start of Subsection 2.2).",
"Nevertheless, we have been able to give a positive answer to the previous conjecture for the Sobolev statement in all cases, and for the NLBVω statement provided Ωsatisfies a certain condition: Theorem 1.6 (The case in which Ωhas infinite measure and ω is bounded away from zero).",
"Assume that Ωis a nonempty open subset of RN that has infinite measure and let p ∈[1, +∞) and ω be an admissible weight function in Ω.",
"If there exists a constant c > 0 such that for all (x, y) ∈Ω× Ωone has c ≤ω(x, y), then we have that W 1,p ω (Ω) = {0}.",
"Moreover, if we further assume that there exists (Ωn)n∈N, a sequence of open subsets of Ωsuch that for each n ∈N the set Ωn is of infinite measure and dist(Ωn, ∂Ω) is positive, then NLBVω(Ω) = {0}.",
The proof of Theorem 1.6 will be given in Section 5.,
"A straightforward consequence of Theorem 1.6 is that W 1,p ω (RN) = NLBVω(RN) = {0} whenever ω is bounded below by a positive constant on RN × RN.",
"The proof of Theorem 1.6 is based on the fact that, for p ̸= +∞, the nonlocal Sobolev seminorm induced by 5 a bounded away from zero weight function of a non-affine function is always infinite whenever Ωhas infinite measure, see Lemma 5.1.",
"With this result, the proof of Theorem 1.6 is obtained directly in the Sobolev case, and is based on a convolution argument in the bounded variation case.",
"In particular, the proof of Theorem 1.6 does not rely on Theorem 1.4 or Corollary 1.5.",
"At this point, one might wonder about the link between the function spaces considered in this paper and the fractional spaces.",
"The latter type of spaces has been widely studied in Sobolev-type frameworks; see Di Nezza, Palatucci and Valdinoci [21] and the references therein.",
"More recently, bounded variation counterparts have been also studied, we refer to Comi and Stefani [18] and Zust [40], see also Antil, Diaz, Jing and Schikorra [2], as well as Bessas et al.",
"[7,8] for applications to image processing.",
"In fact, as already emphasized, our spaces are rather different from the fractional ones.",
"This is essentially due to the fact that, although fractional spaces can also be seen as weighted spaces, such weights are singular on the diagonal of Ω× Ω, in contrast with those we consider in this article which do not have singularities at the interior of Ω× Ω.",
"On a related note, let us mention that we are aware that coordinate-based definitions of fractional operators are known to be flawed, in the sense that they depend on the choice of orthogonal basis (see [35]).",
"However, one can readily prove that this is not the case for the nonlocal operators introduced in this paper.",
"To motivate our next results, let us give a brief overview of the image processing literature that inspired [30, 37], of which our spaces are based on: The seminal paper [34] by Rudin, Osher and Fatemi (see also Chambolle and P.-L. Lions [15]), established total variation as a strong regularization term in image denoising which greatly improved the state of the art at the time, and sparked a significant rise of other total variation-based models.",
"During the last decades, several alternatives to the total variation have been proposed as regularization terms, see, e.g., [4, 6, 9, 16, 28, 32, 36].",
"In the highly influential work [12, 13], Buades, Coll and Morel introduced a nonlocal filter for image denoising, generalizing previous approaches by Yaroslavsky and Eden [38,39].",
"Several variational interpretations of such a filter were given later in [30] as well as by Gilboa and Osher [25,26].",
"In particular, in the latter reference the total variation term in [34] is replaced by a nonlocal term of the type: Z Ω×Ω ω(x, y)|u(y) −u(x)|dxdy, (1.9) where ω is positive and symmetric.",
"In general, nonlocal terms average every pixel with each of the other pixels in the image, rather than simply its neighbors.",
"This is advantageous because, as argued in [12,13,30], natural images are self-similar, in the sense that every small patch has many similar patches in the same image.",
It is then clear that nonlocal regularization terms promote smoothness among similar pixels and thus better preserve the image’s characteristics.,
"The approach in [30] consisted on considering a higher order nonlocal term similar to (1.9), namely Z Ω×Ω |∇u(y) −∇u(x)|dxdy, (1.10) which coincides with NLTV1(u) for u ∈C1(Ω).",
This regularization term was claimed to not suffer from the so-called staircase effect and was showed through numerical experiments to be more advantageous than (1.9) when applied to image deblurring problems.,
A more general variant was proposed in [37] with the scope of addressing problems in image decomposition.,
"More precisely, they considered the following weighted version of (1.10): Z Ω×Ω ω(x, y)|∇u(y) −∇u(x)|dxdy, which, again, corresponds to NLTVω(u) as long as u ∈C1(Ω).",
"In this paper we show the existence and uniqueness to the solution of two minimizing problems which involve the NLBVω and W 1,p ω spaces and their respective seminorms.",
"In contrast to [30,37], our fidelity terms will be general and not fixed.",
"Additionally, our results do not rely on any sort of compact Sobolev-type embeddings, as F is assumed to be convex.",
See Remark 6.1 for additional comments.,
"First, for a fixed 1 < q < +∞, we consider the functional Iω(u) := NLTVω(u) + F(u), (1.11) defined for u ∈NLBVω(Ω) ∩Lq(Ω), where F : Lq(Ω) →[−∞, +∞] is a fidelity term which we assume to be proper, bounded from below, convex, lower semicontinuous (with respect to the Lq norm) and to satisfy the coercivity condition F(u) →+∞ as ∥u∥Lq(Ω) →+∞.",
(1.12) We prove the following: 6 Theorem 1.7 (Existence and uniqueness of minimizers for Iω).,
"Let Ωbe a nonempty open subset of RN of finite measure, ω be an admissible weight function in Ω, 1 < q < +∞, F : Lq(Ω) →[−∞, +∞] be proper, bounded from below, convex, lower semicontinuous and satisfying (1.12), and Iω be as in (1.11).",
"Then, there exists u∗∈NLBVω(u) ∩Lq(Ω) such that Iω(u∗) = m∗:= inf u∈NLBVω(Ω)∩Lq(Ω) Iω(u).",
"Moreover, if F is strictly convex then the minimizer is unique.",
The proof of Theorem 1.7 follows by an application of the Direct Method and will be given in Section 6.,
Such a procedure is only possible when one has established the necessary properties for the underlying space NLBVω(Ω).,
"The q = 1 case is problematic, see Remark 6.1.",
"In the BV case, one might relax the convexity assumption on F and use the compact embeddings into Lq spaces.",
"This is still possible to some extent for NLBVω(Ω), again, see Remark 6.1.",
"Additional assumptions on Ωand ω are needed for such a result, as otherwise the compact embeddings are lost.",
"Indeed, one might think about the limit case in which ω = 0 in Ω× Ω, so that NLBVω(Ω) = L1(Ω).",
"The second functional we consider is Ip ω(u) := Z Ω×Ω |∇ωu|p + F(u), (1.13) defined for u ∈W 1,p ω (Ω) ∩Lq(Ω), where p ∈(1, +∞), p ≤q < +∞and F satisfies the properties described above Theorem 1.7.",
We prove the following: Theorem 1.8 (Existence and uniqueness of minimizers for Ip ω).,
"Let Ωbe a nonempty open subset of RN, p ∈(1, +∞), ω be an admissible weight function in Ω, p ≤q < +∞, F : Lq(Ω) →[−∞, +∞] be proper, bounded from below, convex, lower semicontinuous and satisfying (1.12), and Ip ω be as in (1.13).",
"If q > p, assume furthermore that Ωis of finite measure.",
"Then, there exists u∗,p ∈W 1,p ω (Ω) ∩Lq(Ω) such that Ip ω(u∗,p) = m∗,p := inf u∈W 1,p ω (Ω)∩Lq(Ω) Ip ω(u).",
"Moreover, if F is strictly convex then the minimizer is unique.",
The proof of Theorem 1.8 is given in Section 6.,
"It too follows from an application of the Direct Method, and it relies on a previous study of the properties of W 1,p ω (Ω).",
"The 1 < q < p case can be considered if one modifies the underlying space, and the p = 1 case can be treated as in the bounded variation setting, see Remark 6.2.",
"Moreover, the p = +∞case can also be considered but requires some restrictive conditions on F, see Remark 6.3.",
"As for future work, we hypothesize that the weighted NLBV and nonlocal Sobolev spaces can be advan- tageous in an image restoration context, and we look forward to derive and implement variational models based on the theory developed in this article.",
"In this direction, Theorems 1.7 and 1.8 serve as a preliminary theoretical basis.",
"Moreover, the nonlocal Sobolev space can potentially lead to some new PDE problems, some of which we hope can model real life phenomena governed by nonlocal interactions.",
