By Lemma 2.19 we thus have that u∗∈NLBVω(Ω) and NLTVω(u∗) ≤lim inf n→∞NLTVω(un).,
"Moreover, as F is (strongly) lower semicontinuous and convex, it is weakly lower semicontinuous.",
"This fact, combined with the previous statement and the superadditivity of the limit inferior yields that m∗≤I(u∗) = NLTVω(u∗) + F(u∗) ≤lim inf n→+∞NLTVω(un) + lim inf n→+∞F(un) ≤lim inf n→+∞I(un) = m∗, (6.1) from which the existence statement is proven.",
"If F is strictly convex, so is Iω and the uniqueness statement follows.",
Remark 6.1 (The q = 1 case and relaxing convexity).,
"Notice that if F is assumed to be lower semicontinuous and coercive in L1(Ω), then the existence part of the proof of Theorem 1.7 does not carry out due to the fact that L1(Ω) is not reflexive.",
"Moreover, by considering the limit case ω = 0 in Ω× Ωone then checks that the result might then become false.",
"However, if one assumes that Ωis a bounded extension domain and takes ω such that c ≤ω in Ω×Ωfor a certain constant c > 0, then, by Theorem 1.1 and the well known embeddings between BV and Lp spaces (see, e.g., [1, Corollary 3.49]), one has the continuous embeddings NLBVω(Ω) ,→BV(Ω) ,→Lq(Ω) for all q ∈[1, N/(N −1)), the last one being compact, with the understanding that for N = 1 the critical exponent becomes +∞.",
"Thus, one can then adapt the proof of Theorem 1.7 for this alternative set of assumptions.",
"Indeed, the chain of embeddings yields that a minimizing sequence must have a subsequence strongly converging in L1(Ω), and as F is lower semicontinuous the chain of inequalities (6.1) holds true.",
"Moreover, notice that in this case the convexity assumption on F is not needed for the existence statement.",
Proof of Theorem 1.8.,
"As F is proper and bounded from below, −∞< m∗,p < +∞, where, recall that m∗,p = inf u∈W 1,p ω (Ω)∩Lq(Ω) Ip ω(u).",
