"At batch=1000, the GPU achieves 4,565.8 samples/s with 11.4 ms p99 latency, whereas Spark (8-node) sustains only ∼877–893 samples/s with ∼1,140 ms p99 latency (Table I).",
"Figure 2 shows that the gap persists across small (HR), medium (UCI Adult), and temporal (BLS) datasets; the ad- vantage widens as batch size grows.",
"Despite a higher hourly rate for the GPU instance, cost per 1K inferences drops by up to 90% because the GPU completes work significantly faster, with latency reductions of up to two orders of mag- nitude; the cost break-even occurs around batch ≥200 and becomes strongly favorable beyond 500 (Table V).",
"CPU-only baselines further contextualize this result (Table II): TabNet- GPU delivers 2.1× the throughput of the best CPU tree model (LightGBM) and 11.8× TabNet-CPU, while also offering substantially lower latency.",
"Latency remains tightly bounded under load for the GPU pipeline, with stable p95/p99 values, in contrast to Spark, which shows heavy-tailed variance due to distributed coordination and shuffle overheads.",
"GPU utilization peaks at 78%, providing headroom for bursts and indicating efficient hardware usage even at peak throughput (batch size = 5000)2.",
Three observations.,
(1) Scaling regime.,
"The GPU exhibits near-superlinear scaling with batch size (higher SM occupancy 2Measured using NVIDIA DCGM Exporter TABLE II CPU VS GPU PERFORMANCE COMPARISON (STEADY-STATE, BATCH SIZE = 1000) Configuration Throughput Latency Cost/1K (samples/s) (ms) (USD) TabNet-CPU (8 cores) 387.2 ± 4.3% 258.4 ± 5.1% 0.0003 XGBoost (8 cores) 1,842.6 ± 2.8% 54.3 ± 3.2% 0.0001 LightGBM (8 cores) 2,156.3 ± 2.5% 46.4 ± 2.9% 0.0001 TabNet-GPU (T4) 4,565.8 ± 1.7% 11.4 ± 2.5% 0.0001 and amortized kernel launch), whereas Spark shows dimin- ishing returns due to per-batch coordination and serialization overheads.",
(2) Tail behavior.,
"The GPU’s tails are short and stable; Spark’s p95/p99 inflate under contention and recovery, which is detrimental for interactive SLAs.",
(3) Cost inflection.,
"When batches are ≥200, GPU per-1K cost undercuts Spark because time-to-completion, not hourly price, outperforms spend; beyond 500, GPU becomes the more cost-effective option.",
Why this happens (systems perspective).,
"First, the GPU vertically parallelizes TabNet’s tensor kernels (matrix multi- plies, attention masks) with high arithmetic intensity, so more of the wall-clock is useful compute rather than coordination.",
"Second, the serverless design collapses the data path: no dis- tributed shuffle, no JVM deserialization fan-out, and no multi- hop RPC.",
"Third, amortization effects dominate on the GPU: loading the model into VRAM and reusing CUDA contexts converts many fixed costs into one-time warm costs, whereas Spark repeatedly pays scheduling, barrier synchronization, and executor wake-up penalties.",
Practical guidance.,
Use the GPU serverless pipeline when (i) batch sizes routinely exceed 200 or when p99<50 ms is a hard SLO; (ii) workloads are bursty and benefit from scale-to-zero and fast warm invocations; (iii) explainability is needed without sacrificing latency (paired with TabNet’s masks).,
Prefer CPU/Spark only when (i) batches are tiny and cold-start outperforms end-to-end time; (ii) the workload is dominated by massive joins/aggregations over multi-terabyte tables; or (iii) long-running streaming jobs require complex stateful operators that are outside the request/response infer- ence pattern.,
RQ-1 Insight.,
"A single-node, GPU-backed serverless pipeline is the superior performance–cost point for moderate-to-large batch inference: up to 4.5× higher throughput and 98× lower latency than Spark while cutting cost per 1K inferences by as much as 90%.",
This establishes GPU serverless as a practical production default for latency-sensitive enterprise analytics.,
B. RQ-2: Security Overhead Analysis Motivation.,
"In regulated domains such as government HR analytics, strong compliance requirements (IL4/FIPS) mandate end-to-end security guarantees, including mutual TLS, token- based authentication, and immutable audit logs.",
"While these mechanisms ensure confidentiality and accountability, they also introduce additional cryptographic operations and request processing stages.",
"The key question is whether enabling full compliance breaks latency SLAs (e.g., p95/p99 < 20 ms) or reduces throughput to the point of making GPU serverless unattractive.",
Table III quantifies the incremental latency cost of each control.,
"Enabling mTLS increases p99 latency by 2.8 ms, JWT validation adds another 1.6 ms, and audit logging contributes 1.2 ms.",
"The full IL4 stack therefore introduces a cumulative overhead of 5.7 ms, raising latency at batch=1000 from 11.4 ms (baseline) to 17.1 ms. Throughput declines mod- estly from 4,566 to 4,218 samples/s but remains more than 60× faster than Spark baselines.",
"Figure 3 compares Spark and GPU serverless across batch sizes, both with and without IL4 controls: even under full compliance, GPU latency remains one to two orders of magnitude lower than Spark’s.",
"Impor- tantly, latency tails remain stable, with p95/p99 increasing only slightly under IL4 enforcement.",
